1
00:00:00,584 --> 00:00:03,312
Every day, payment apps like Paytm handle

2
00:00:03,312 --> 00:00:05,296
millions of support requests.

3
00:00:05,296 --> 00:00:06,846
A UPI payment that failed,

4
00:00:06,846 --> 00:00:08,493
but still left the bank.

5
00:00:08,493 --> 00:00:10,163
A refund for a broken order.

6
00:00:10,163 --> 00:00:12,543
A merchant settlement that came up short.

7
00:00:12,543 --> 00:00:14,106
Chatbots can only talk,

8
00:00:14,106 --> 00:00:15,500
so tickets pile up.

9
00:00:15,500 --> 00:00:17,892
But an AI agent that can move money

10
00:00:17,892 --> 00:00:19,744
can also be talked into moving it.

11
00:00:20,632 --> 00:00:23,120
Meet Sakshi, Hindi for witness:

12
00:00:23,120 --> 00:00:25,560
a support agent designed to be audited,

13
00:00:25,560 --> 00:00:26,882
not just smart.

14
00:00:26,882 --> 00:00:29,034
The AI proposes an action.

15
00:00:29,034 --> 00:00:30,344
A policy engine,

16
00:00:30,344 --> 00:00:31,690
written in plain code,

17
00:00:31,690 --> 00:00:34,995
verifies it. Only then does the action run,

18
00:00:34,995 --> 00:00:36,666
and every step is sealed

19
00:00:36,666 --> 00:00:38,578
in a hash-chained audit ledger.

20
00:00:39,480 --> 00:00:41,295
Here is the live stage.

21
00:00:41,295 --> 00:00:43,795
Riya's tote bag arrived broken,

22
00:00:43,795 --> 00:00:46,812
so she asks for a 499 rupee refund.

23
00:00:46,812 --> 00:00:47,533
On the right,

24
00:00:47,533 --> 00:00:49,396
supervisors watch the agent's

25
00:00:49,396 --> 00:00:50,646
reasoning stream in.

26
00:00:50,646 --> 00:00:51,511
It checks the order,

27
00:00:51,511 --> 00:00:53,182
proposes a refund,

28
00:00:53,182 --> 00:00:54,793
and the policy engine allows it,

29
00:00:54,793 --> 00:00:56,692
because 499 is under the

30
00:00:56,692 --> 00:00:58,891
500 rupee autonomy limit.

31
00:00:58,891 --> 00:01:00,117
Riya gets a receipt,

32
00:01:00,117 --> 00:01:02,018
with a ten-minute undo window.

33
00:01:02,912 --> 00:01:04,246
Anyone can ask why.

34
00:01:04,246 --> 00:01:05,568
The answer is built only

35
00:01:05,568 --> 00:01:06,794
from the audit record:

36
00:01:06,794 --> 00:01:08,044
the rules it cited,

37
00:01:08,044 --> 00:01:09,451
its confidence score,

38
00:01:09,451 --> 00:01:12,876
and the block's SHA-256 hash,

39
00:01:12,876 --> 00:01:14,945
linked to the hash of the block before it.

40
00:01:15,844 --> 00:01:18,320
Next, Riya writes in Hinglish.

41
00:01:18,320 --> 00:01:20,099
Her UPI payment failed,

42
00:01:20,099 --> 00:01:23,440
but 499 rupees still left her bank.

43
00:01:23,440 --> 00:01:25,207
Sakshi replies in Hinglish,

44
00:01:25,207 --> 00:01:27,106
sees the payment is inside

45
00:01:27,106 --> 00:01:29,823
the 48-hour auto-reversal window,

46
00:01:29,823 --> 00:01:32,168
and shows her exactly where the money is.

47
00:01:33,060 --> 00:01:34,671
Now a bigger ask:

48
00:01:34,671 --> 00:01:38,577
earbuds worth 1,499 rupees.

49
00:01:38,577 --> 00:01:40,296
That is above the agent's limit,

50
00:01:40,296 --> 00:01:41,498
and over the daily cap

51
00:01:41,498 --> 00:01:43,998
of 1,000 rupees per customer.

52
00:01:43,998 --> 00:01:45,861
So the agent cannot pay out.

53
00:01:45,861 --> 00:01:48,156
It sends the case to a supervisor,

54
00:01:48,156 --> 00:01:49,382
and tells Riya she will

55
00:01:49,382 --> 00:01:51,307
not have to repeat herself.

56
00:01:52,220 --> 00:01:54,588
The supervisor gets a handoff packet:

57
00:01:54,588 --> 00:01:56,090
the order, the amount,

58
00:01:56,090 --> 00:01:57,785
today's refund total,

59
00:01:57,785 --> 00:01:58,831
a trust score,

60
00:01:58,831 --> 00:02:00,453
and a suggested next step.

61
00:02:00,453 --> 00:02:01,691
One click approves it.

62
00:02:01,691 --> 00:02:03,218
The refund runs,

63
00:02:03,218 --> 00:02:04,576
and the approval is recorded

64
00:02:04,576 --> 00:02:06,140
under the supervisor's name.

65
00:02:07,024 --> 00:02:08,106
Now an attack.

66
00:02:08,106 --> 00:02:10,341
Someone claims to be Paytm's CEO,

67
00:02:10,341 --> 00:02:12,986
and orders a 6,000 rupee refund.

68
00:02:12,986 --> 00:02:14,861
The language model can be fooled,

69
00:02:14,861 --> 00:02:17,120
but the policy engine cannot.

70
00:02:17,120 --> 00:02:20,498
6,000 is above the 5,000 rupee ceiling,

71
00:02:20,498 --> 00:02:22,349
so the refund is blocked,

72
00:02:22,349 --> 00:02:24,381
and handed to a human specialist.

73
00:02:25,284 --> 00:02:25,981
Attack Mode has a

74
00:02:25,981 --> 00:02:28,145
library of scripted attacks:

75
00:02:28,145 --> 00:02:30,392
prompt injection, fake authority,

76
00:02:30,392 --> 00:02:32,436
sob stories, jailbreaks,

77
00:02:32,436 --> 00:02:34,851
forged tokens and limit smuggling.

78
00:02:34,851 --> 00:02:36,438
Each one hits the same wall.

79
00:02:36,438 --> 00:02:38,302
No prompt can talk its way around code.

80
00:02:39,176 --> 00:02:42,217
Every proposal, verdict and approval lands

81
00:02:42,217 --> 00:02:43,299
in the audit chain.

82
00:02:43,299 --> 00:02:46,111
Verify it, and every hash checks out.

83
00:02:46,111 --> 00:02:48,275
Now we tamper with the latest record,

84
00:02:48,275 --> 00:02:50,426
directly in the database.

85
00:02:50,426 --> 00:02:51,856
The check fails at once,

86
00:02:51,856 --> 00:02:53,769
and points to exactly that block.

87
00:02:54,676 --> 00:02:56,563
Supervisors tune the limits

88
00:02:56,563 --> 00:02:57,993
with the autonomy dial,

89
00:02:57,993 --> 00:03:00,469
but never past the hard safety caps.

90
00:03:00,469 --> 00:03:01,659
Before committing,

91
00:03:01,659 --> 00:03:04,147
the simulator replays past cases

92
00:03:04,147 --> 00:03:05,385
under the new limits,

93
00:03:05,385 --> 00:03:07,236
without calling the model again.

94
00:03:07,236 --> 00:03:09,712
Lowering the limit to 200 rupees sends

95
00:03:09,712 --> 00:03:12,310
one more case to a supervisor.

96
00:03:13,212 --> 00:03:16,421
And the eval suite runs 29 cases:

97
00:03:16,421 --> 00:03:18,609
tier boundaries, velocity caps,

98
00:03:18,609 --> 00:03:21,493
UPI edge cases and attacks.

99
00:03:21,493 --> 00:03:22,948
All 29 pass,

100
00:03:22,948 --> 00:03:25,185
with zero unsafe actions.

101
00:03:26,060 --> 00:03:26,998
Under the hood,

102
00:03:26,998 --> 00:03:29,029
a React and TypeScript front end

103
00:03:29,029 --> 00:03:31,529
talks to a FastAPI back end.

104
00:03:31,529 --> 00:03:33,897
The orchestrator asks a pluggable LLM

105
00:03:33,897 --> 00:03:35,543
adapter for the next step.

106
00:03:35,543 --> 00:03:36,841
In this demo it runs

107
00:03:36,841 --> 00:03:38,656
the built-in offline engine,

108
00:03:38,656 --> 00:03:40,639
so no API keys are needed.

109
00:03:40,639 --> 00:03:42,887
Every proposed tool call goes

110
00:03:42,887 --> 00:03:44,293
to the policy engine,

111
00:03:44,293 --> 00:03:46,505
which loads its rules from a YAML file.

112
00:03:46,505 --> 00:03:48,392
Allowed calls reach mock Paytm

113
00:03:48,392 --> 00:03:50,784
services backed by SQLite,

114
00:03:50,784 --> 00:03:52,010
and every step streams to

115
00:03:52,010 --> 00:03:54,667
the supervisor over server-sent events.

116
00:03:55,564 --> 00:03:58,184
The engine returns one of four verdicts.

117
00:03:58,184 --> 00:03:59,603
Under 500 rupees,

118
00:03:59,603 --> 00:04:01,970
allow. Up to 5,000,

119
00:04:01,970 --> 00:04:04,146
a supervisor must approve.

120
00:04:04,146 --> 00:04:07,115
Above that, escalate to a specialist.

121
00:04:07,115 --> 00:04:08,497
And a duplicate refund,

122
00:04:08,497 --> 00:04:10,119
or someone else's order,

123
00:04:10,119 --> 00:04:11,598
is denied outright.

124
00:04:11,598 --> 00:04:14,352
The model never picks the tier. Code does.

125
00:04:15,216 --> 00:04:17,380
Each audit block records who acted,

126
00:04:17,380 --> 00:04:18,281
what they did,

127
00:04:18,281 --> 00:04:19,663
and which rule applied,

128
00:04:19,663 --> 00:04:21,791
plus the hash of the block before it,

129
00:04:21,791 --> 00:04:25,072
all hashed with SHA-256.

130
00:04:25,072 --> 00:04:26,610
Change a single field,

131
00:04:26,610 --> 00:04:29,122
and that block's hash no longer matches.

132
00:04:29,122 --> 00:04:31,094
Verification walks the chain from the

133
00:04:31,094 --> 00:04:33,126
genesis block to the tip.

134
00:04:34,028 --> 00:04:36,708
Sakshi. The AI proposes,

135
00:04:36,708 --> 00:04:39,629
code verifies, and the ledger remembers.

136
00:04:39,629 --> 00:04:41,769
Built by Team XLR8:

137
00:04:41,769 --> 00:04:44,570
Siddhant Shukla and Raunak Kumar Gupta.
